Threads stopped by Nebula, not by a signal (docs/w4.md W4c). On a POSIX
system WTF suspends a thread for the collector by sending it SIGUSR1 and
copying its registers in the handler; Kosmos has no signals, and Nebula
suspends a thread of one's own process, reads its registers and lets it
go (SYS_THREAD_SUSPEND, docs/w2.md part 3), which the POSIX layer offers
as pthread_suspend_np, pthread_getregs_np and pthread_resume_np. So
OS(KOSMOS) takes Darwin's shape - the kernel stops the thread - with
those calls, installs no handler, has no Thread::signal, and keeps
WTF's signal-handler table in kosmos/SignalsKosmos.cpp, where nothing
is ever delivered to it.
(The Kosmos port's WTF sources name it: PlatformKosmos.cmake.)

